Description: CVE-2026-78376 - Fix use-after-free of JSCValue function parameters
 Processing malicious web content could trigger memory corruption because the
 JSValueRef arguments accumulated during variadic parameter conversion in the
 JavaScriptCore GLib API (jsObjectCall / jscValueCallFunction) were held in a
 plain Vector<JSValueRef> that did not root its contents for the garbage
 collector. Converting a later parameter can allocate and trigger a collection
 that frees an earlier argument still needed by the call (CWE-416).
 .
 This backports upstream WebKit commit 9fa946d9a72f (319867@main, bug 322290)
 to webkitgtk 2.52.6. The upstream fix switches the accumulator to
 JSC::MarkedVector<JSValueRef>, but that generalized template does not compile
 against 2.52.6 (MarkedVector<T>::append() calls JSValue::encode(v), which has
 no JSValueRef overload). The GC-rooting guarantee is instead reimplemented
 with the target-native, GC-rooted JSC::MarkedJSValueRefArray. Because that
 container needs its element count at construction, jscValueCallFunction's
 variadic va_arg loop is restructured into a two-pass count-then-fill approach
 (a va_copy pre-pass counts the parameters via G_VALUE_COLLECT_SKIP). The
 std::span<const JSValueRef> signature cleanup of jsObjectCall is ported as-is,
 and the three fixed-arity call sites pass their (already-rooted) Vector via
 span(). A regression test (testJSCFunctionCallParameterLifetime) exercising
 the parameter lifetime is added.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/commit/9fa946d9a72f9ebd5703e0a301849482f6c8463b
Bug: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=322290
Author: Justin Michaud <jmichaud@igalia.com>
Last-Update: 2026-08-28
---
